{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Matrix.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Trace","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":20,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.weighted_letter_weights_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.weighted_letter_weights_sum","kind":"theorem","line":267,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingConvexity.lean#L267-L273","doc":"Its weights sum to one — the letter is an affine combination, and a convex\none exactly when both weights are nonnegative. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.weighted_letter","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.weighted_letter","kind":"theorem","line":260,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingConvexity.lean#L260-L265","doc":"The colour-weighted letter `σ - h(1/2 - δ/4)` in two-crossing coordinates. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.averaged_letter","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.averaged_letter","kind":"theorem","line":254,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingConvexity.lean#L254-L258","doc":"The averaged crossing is the midpoint of the two crossings. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.trace_split_of_add_eq_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.trace_split_of_add_eq_one","kind":"theorem","line":236,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingConvexity.lean#L236-L241","doc":"`P + Q = 1` splits any character additively.  The numerical instance is the\npair of Lagrange spectral projectors of `sigma_1`, whose measured ratios are\n`1.103672165665516` and `-0.103672165665516`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.trace_mul_scalar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.trace_mul_scalar","kind":"theorem","line":226,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingConvexity.lean#L226-L232","doc":"**Central multiplication rescales the character.**  This is the whole\ncontent of \"the ell-1 ceiling bounds precisely the centre\": the ceiling already\nranges over per-block coefficients, and a central `Z = c • 1` only supplies one\nmore. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.crossingCoord_snd_neg_of_two_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.crossingCoord_snd_neg_of_two_le","kind":"theorem","line":205,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingConvexity.lean#L205-L213","doc":"**Composing crossings leaves the cone.**  For `k ≥ 2` and `h > 0`, the\n`s⁻¹` coordinate of `s^k` is strictly negative, so `s^k` is not a nonnegative\ncombination of the two crossings. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.pow_coords_fst_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.pow_coords_fst_pos","kind":"theorem","line":187,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingConvexity.lean#L187-L203","doc":"The constant coordinate of `s^{k+2}` is strictly positive when `h > 0`.\nProved together with the next index so the two-step recursion closes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.sq_crossingCoord_snd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.sq_crossingCoord_snd","kind":"theorem","line":157,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingConvexity.lean#L157-L180","doc":"The `k = 2` case in closed form: `s² = h·s + 1` has `b = -1/h`.\n\n**Hypothesis dropped and section moved, 2026-09-05.** This was stated in\n`section Ordered` as `{h : K} (hh : 0 < h)`, and `hh` was never used: the\nproof is `rfl`, because `toCrossingCoords h c₁ cσ` is *defined* as\n`(cσ + c₁/h, -(c₁/h))`, so the second component at `c₁ = 1` is `-(1/h)`\nsyntactically, for every `h` — including `h = 0`, where both sides are\nLean's junk `-(1/0)`. Carrying `0 < h` made the theorem strictly weaker\nthan what was proved and made it look as though positivity were doing work\nsomewhere. It also forced the whole statement into the ordered section,\nrequiring `[LinearOrder K] [IsStrictOrderedRing K]` for an identity that\nneeds only `[Field K]` — the §7c defect of inheriting archimedean structure\na statement does not use.\n\nContrast `crossingCoord_snd_neg_of_two_le` immediately below in `Ordered`,\nwhich genuinely needs `0 < h`: its conclusion is an *inequality*, and the\npositivity is what drives the `pow_coords` induction. The two sat adjacent\nwith the same binder, which is presumably how the decorative one arrived.\n\nCaught by `proof-no-unused-hypothesis-rfl` (ledger entry at the old line\n191). No consumers: the declaration is referenced nowhere else in the\ncorpus, so widening it breaks nothing. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.pow_coords_spec","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.pow_coords_spec","kind":"theorem","line":136,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingConvexity.lean#L136-L155","doc":"The recursion reproduces the powers of `s`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.pow_coords_succ_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.pow_coords_succ_succ","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingConvexity.lean#L131-L134","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.pow_coords_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.pow_coords_one","kind":"theorem","line":129,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingConvexity.lean#L129-L129","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.pow_coords_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.pow_coords_zero","kind":"theorem","line":128,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingConvexity.lean#L128-L128","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.pow_coords","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.pow_coords","kind":"def","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingConvexity.lean#L121-L126","doc":"`(pow_coords h k).1 + (pow_coords h k).2 * s = s ^ k`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.crossingCoord_snd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.crossingCoord_snd","kind":"theorem","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingConvexity.lean#L112-L114","doc":"The `s⁻¹` coordinate is `-c₁/h`; it depends only on the constant term. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.toCrossingCoords_spec","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.toCrossingCoords_spec","kind":"theorem","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingConvexity.lean#L103-L110","doc":"**The coordinate change is correct.**  With `a, b` as above,\n`a·s + b·s⁻¹ = c₁ + c_σ·s`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.toCrossingCoords","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.toCrossingCoords","kind":"def","line":100,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingConvexity.lean#L100-L101","doc":"The change of coordinates: `c₁ + c_σ·s` written over the two crossings. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.crossing_expand","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.crossing_expand","kind":"theorem","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingConvexity.lean#L90-L93","doc":"A combination of the two crossings, rewritten in the `{1, s}` basis. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.opp_mul_self","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.opp_mul_self","kind":"theorem","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingConvexity.lean#L83-L88","doc":"`opp` really is the inverse crossing: `s⁻¹ · s = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.opp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.opp","kind":"def","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingConvexity.lean#L80-L81","doc":"The opposite crossing.  Under `IsCrossing h s` this is the inverse of `s`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.IsCrossing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.CrossingConvexity.IsCrossing","kind":"def","line":77,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/CrossingConvexity.lean#L77-L78","doc":"The Hecke quadratic relation `s² = h·s + 1` satisfied by a crossing. ","signatureChunk":0}]}